<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Matei Benescu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Groningen
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">matei.benescu22@gmail.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ivo Pascal de Jong
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Groningen
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ivo.de.jong@rug.nl</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">With the emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs), new methods in Information Retrieval are available in which relevance is estimated directly through language understanding and reasoning, instead of embedding similarity. We argue that similarity is a <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">short-sighted</span> interpretation of relevance, and that LLM-Based Relevance Judgment Systems (LLM-RJS) (with reasoning) have potential to outperform Neural Embedding Retrieval Systems (NERS) by overcoming this limitation. Using the TREC-DL 2019 passage retrieval dataset, we compare various LLM-RJS with NERS, but observe no noticeable improvement.
Subsequently, we analyze the impact of reasoning by comparing LLM-RJS with and without reasoning. We find that human annotations also suffer from short-sightedness, and that false-positives in the reasoning LLM-RJS are primarily mistakes in annotations due to short-sightedness. We conclude that LLM-RJS do have the ability to address the short-sightedness limitation in NERS, but that this cannot be evaluated with standard annotated relevance datasets.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Information Retrieval (IR) is the task of finding relevant information in a pool of numerous documents, where a document can be any retrievable unit like a webpage or text fragment. IR methods have constantly been evolving (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazi et al., 2025</a></cite>). Vector Space Models (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salton, 1965</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salton et al., 1975</a></cite>) use sparse vector representations of term weights, where each weight represents the frequency of the term in the given document. Documents are ordered by relevance to the query, by the means of an ordering function, such as the BM25 (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robertson et al., 2009</a>, Chapter 3.4</cite>). Modern IR methods rely on dense vector representations of text, known as “embeddings", which are learned by Neural Networks (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2013</a></cite>). We will refer to these methods as Neural Embeddings Retrieval Systems (NERS). In NERS, documents are ordered by their similarity with the query, which is given by a distance metric such as Euclidean Distance, or Cosine Similarity (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Dong, 2020</a></cite>). <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karpukhin et al., 2020</a></cite> showed that NERS outperform sparse vector representation approaches.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.08077v1/LLM-IR.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/337;" width="476" height="337" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Motivation for LLM-RJS. The query and document are not similar, so embedding similarity is too short-sighted for IR. The document is the correct answer, but requires reasoning about relevance to identify as a match.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have been proposed as a different approach to judging how relevant a document is to a query (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">MacAvaney and Soldaini, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Faggioli et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abbasiantaeb et al., 2024</a></cite>). <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a></cite> showed that LLMs’ relevance judgments are similar to those of humans. This has the potential to outperform NERS models, as NERS models are limited by retrieving documents that are <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">similar</span> to the query, but relevant documents are not always similar. An example of NERS being short-sighted is shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the scope of this paper, we will call a system that uses an LLMs verbalized relevance score for matching an <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-Based Relevance Judgment System</span> (LLM-RJS). While LLM-RJS have been shown to be effective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al. (2024)</a></cite>, to the best of our knowledge a 1-to-1 comparison against NERS is missing.
Additionally, IR applications of reasoning models that are equipped with Chain of Thought (CoT) capabilities, such as GPT-o1 (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaech et al., 2024</a></cite>) remain unexplored. As reasoning capabilities become cheaper and available in open models (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a></cite>), examining its impact on document relevance judgments becomes increasingly important and broadly applicable.
We aim to build on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a></cite> by comparing LLM-RJS to NERS. Furthermore, we explore the effects of reasoning capabilities of LLMs in the task of document relevance estimation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">With this, we make the following contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate NERS and multiple LLM-RJS models on the TREC-DL 2019 passage retrieval benchmark to compare their performances in relevance ranking. We see that top LLMs perform on par with NERS, but do not yield demonstrably better rankings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the impact of “reasoning effort” for LLM-RJS at directly estimating the relevance scores. We propose that this can help to overcome the short-sightedness of NERS. We find that adding reasoning results in higher relevance ratings that the human relevance scores, and that the LLM-RJS overall shows higher relevance ratings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explain the difference in relevance ratings as short-sightedness in the ground-truth annotations, showing examples of documents that have low relevance ratings in the human annotation, but that are highly relevant. From the 94 ‘false positives’, 89 are actually mistakes in the annotation because the passages are relevant but not similar.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude that the empirical capabilities of zero-shot methods for Information Retrieval may be underestimated due to short-sighted annotations, making short-sighted similarity models appear better than more capable reasoning models. LLM-RJS have the ability to overcome the short-sighted limitation of NERS, but this does not lead to better metrics when evaluated on human annotations.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
