</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe previous research on NERS and LLM-RJS models. While NERS stem from a long line of existing research in text embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mikolov et al. (2013)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pennington et al. (2014)</a></cite>, we specifically focus on embeddings from Neural Networks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">NERS</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Neural Embedding Retrieval Systems (NERS) rely on pre-trained language models to create a dense vector representation of text. These language models are Neural Networks trained on some other such as Masked Language Modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>)</cite> or Contrastive Learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2021)</a></cite>. The penulatimate layer of the Neural Network therefore has a dense representation of the semantic meaning of the text, capturing properties like synonymy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karpukhin et al. (2020)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">NERS models work by computing the embedding representation of the query and the document, and evaluating the distance between them, typically through cosine similarity. When a document and a query describe similar concepts they get a higher similarity score. Document relevance is therefore ranked by similarity to the query.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We consider this a fundamental limitation of IR systems that rely on embedding similarities. While similarity between query and document is a good heuristic for relevance, it is not exactly the same. When the document answers the question but does not repeat content from the query (as the example in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), it is not similar, but can still be highly relevant. Even with ever-improving embedding models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trabelsi et al. (2021)</a></cite>, this necessarily gives a limitation of NERS.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM-RJS</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Model-Based Relevance Judgement Systems (LLM-RJS) instead use the generated answers from an LLM to establish relevance. In this work, the LLM is prompted with query and document, and instructed to quantify how “relevant” the document is in line with the human annotations, though many variations for prompting exist <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arabzadeh and Clarke (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Faggioli et al., 2023</a></cite> found that LLMs can perform comparably to humans in identifying irrelevant information but struggled to consistently recognize relevant pieces of information. In their results GPT-3.5 showed a higher false negative rate than humans.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluating the goodness of document relevance assigned by LLMs can be directly compared to human-labeled query-document pairs are used as the “ground truth". However, humans are not consistent in rating document relevance (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bailey et al., 2008</a></cite>). To evaluate LLMs fairly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a></cite> posed the issue as “if
LLMs produce labels that agree with humans to the same extent
that humans agree with each other (human-to-human range), we can conclude that they are
sufficiently reliable for use". Their results show that larger LLMs, such as GPT-4, GPT-4o, and LLaMA70B give results in the human-to-human range. Only GPT-4 and GPT-4o were robust to the prompt variations. However, this perspective cannot show when LLMs might outperform humans in the ability to estimate relevance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Because LLM-RJS systems are zero-shot models and are not trained as supervised learning systems on the human annotations, we should consider that it has the potential to outperform human annotations. Additionally, with reasoning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we can generate reasoning processes that show relevance in a way that has more depth than simple similarity scores, and might have more depth than the human labels.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Dataset</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TREC (Text REtrieval Conference) datasets are commonly used in the IR field (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">MacAvaney and Soldaini, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Faggioli et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a></cite>). Similarly to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a></cite>, we use queries and passages from the test set of the the passage retrieval task dataset of the Deep Learning Track of TREC 2019 (TREC-DL 2019) (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees et al., 2020</a></cite>). TREC-DL 2019 contains triples of form (query, passage, relevance score).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The passage corpora of TREC-DL 2019 are derived from the MS MARCO dataset. MS MARCO is a large-scale dataset of real web search queries and web text originally sampled from Bing search logs. Queries in MS MARCO are real user queries and passages are extracted from web documents obtained via Bing search results. Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives some examples of (query, passage, relevance) triples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The relevance scores of TREC-DL 2019 are evaluated by human assessors. The scores (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees et al., 2020</a>, Section 2.2</cite>) are integer values using a 4-point scale (0-3) where:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perfectly relevant:</span> The passage is dedicated to the query and contains the exact answer.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Highly relevant:</span> The passage has some answer for the query, but the answer may be a bit unclear, or hidden amongst extraneous information.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Related:</span> The passage seems related to the query but does not answer it.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">0.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Irrelevant:</span> The passage has nothing to do with the query.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For both experiments, the LLMs are instructed to only output the relevance score of the (query, document) pair. Any response that could not be casted to an integer between 0 and 3 was considered a failed response. All others are successes. For each model, the (query, document) pairs were divided into successes and failures. The intersection of all successful pairs was kept for final analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment 1</h2>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.08077v1/experiment1_overview.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/130;" width="476" height="130"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of Experiment 1. Documents are ranked (ordered) based on the "relevance score" with respect to the query assigned by each system. The rankings are compared to the ranking yielded by humans labelers, via NDCG. The mean NDCG is used as final evaluation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate whether LLM-RJS can outperform NERS, and therefore attempt to address the short-sightedness issue of NERS, we compare the performance of NERS with LLM-RJS in document ranking. As a baseline we evaluate the BM25 model. We first describe the implementation details of the three models, and then how they are evaluated. The overall setup of Experiment 1 is demonstrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Experiment 1 ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM-RJS</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All models were asked to estimate the relevance of (query,
passage) pairs on a 4-point scale pair, using the prompt found in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompt ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. The prompt describes the task and provides the relevance score convention. The models were also given examples of (query, document, relevance) triples, as examples are known to enhance LLM performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2020)</a></cite>. We use both closed state-of-the-art (at the moment of writing), as well as open-weights models. Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ LLM-RJS ‣ 4 Experiment 1 ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides an overview of the LLM models evaluated. The temperature hyperparameter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ackley et al., 1985</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peeperkorn et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, controls the amount of “randomness” in LLM token generation. The temperature was set to 0 for all models, to ensure reproducibility and because higher temperatures have been shown to have a moderate correlation with incoherence (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peeperkorn et al., 2024</a></cite>).
The reasoning efforts of GPT-5.1 was set to ‘‘none’’. The open-weights models were run using Ollama<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            https://ollama.com/</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Provider</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open Weights</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
