</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>LLMs used in Experiment 1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ollama (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta (2024a)</a></cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.08077v1/NDCG_violin.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:284/208;" width="284" height="208"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.08077v1/NDCG_5_violin.svg" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:164/208;" width="164" height="208"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.08077v1/NDCG_10_violin.svg" id="S4.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:164/208;" width="164" height="208"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Performance of <span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#1F77B4;">LLM-RJS</span> and <span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF7F0E;">NERS</span> models in relevance ranking, measured by NDCG@k. Top LLM-RJS models perform on par with NERS models, but do not appear to outperform them. Results are in line with previous research from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hofstätter et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xing et al. (2023)</a></cite>, reporting mean NDCG@10 of 0.72 and 0.73 respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Neural Embeddings Retrieval Systems:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For NERS, we used a closed model and an open-weights one. The closed model, gemini-embedding001, is the state-of-the-art from Google, showed to outperform most of the current embedding models (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2025</a></cite>). The open model is nomic-embed-text:v1.5<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            https://huggingface.co/nomic-ai/nomic-embed-text-v1.5</span></span></span>. It is an extension of nomic-embed-text:v1 (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nussbaum et al., 2024</a></cite>) that uses Matryoshka Representation Learning (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kusupati et al., 2022</a></cite>). The base model, nomic-embed-text:v1, showed better performance (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nussbaum et al., 2024</a></cite>) than similarly sized embedding models and established models like GloVe (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pennington et al., 2014</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">BM25 baseline:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">BM25 is a probabilistic scoring function that ranks documents based on query term frequencies while balancing the effects of term frequency and document length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The BM25 scoring function is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.Ex1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:25.6pt;vertical-align:-14.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(54.6pt,-4.1pt) scale(1.46327266457847,1.46327266457847) ;">
<p id="S4.Ex1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BM25}(d,q)=\sum_{t\in q}\mathrm{IDF}(t)\frac{f_{t,d}(k_{1}+1)}{f_{t,d}+k_{1}\left(1-b+b\frac{|d|}{\mathrm{avgdl}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>BM25</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>IDF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>avgdl</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BM25}(d,q)=\sum_{t\in q}\mathrm{IDF}(t)\frac{f_{t,d}(k_{1}+1)}{f_{t,d}+k_{1}\left(1-b+b\frac{|d|}{\mathrm{avgdl}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the query, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t,d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the frequency of term <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in document <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the degree of term-frequency saturation, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d|</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of document <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">avgdl</span> is the average document length in the collection, and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> controls the strength of document length normalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The inverse document frequency (IDF) reduces the weight of common terms while increasing the importance of rarer terms across the collection:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{IDF}(t)=\log\frac{N-n_{t}+0.5}{n_{t}+0.5}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>IDF</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{IDF}(t)=\log\frac{N-n_{t}+0.5}{n_{t}+0.5}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of documents and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of documents containing term <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees et al., 2020</a></cite>, BM25 with the default parameters (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}=1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}=1.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=0.75</annotation></semantics></math>) was chosen as a baseline ranking system.
The rankings yielded by both LLM-RJS and NERS will be compared to the one of BM25.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model evaluation:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The relevance estimates from NERS and BM25 cannot be directly compared to ground truth relevance scores, due to different representations of relevance. Instead, the document rankings, i.e. the set of documents ordered by their relevance to the query, yielded by the different systems can be directly compared. 
<br class="ltx_break">The Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG) (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2013</a></cite>) is a measure of ranking quality, which we will use to compare the rankings obtained by different retrieval systems. 
<br class="ltx_break">NDCG is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{NDCG}(A)=\frac{\mathrm{DCG}(A)}{\mathrm{DCG}(I)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>NDCG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>DCG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>DCG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{NDCG}(A)=\frac{\mathrm{DCG}(A)}{\mathrm{DCG}(I)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\quad\mathrm{DCG}(R)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{h(d_{i})}{\log(i+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>DCG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\quad\mathrm{DCG}(R)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{h(d_{i})}{\log(i+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal ranking and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(d_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(d_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the relevance of document <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> at position <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in the ranking. NDCG ranges from 0 to 1, with 1 corresponding to the ideal ranking induced by human relevance judgments.
<br class="ltx_break">NDCG@k is NDCG evaluated for the sub-ranking that consists of the first <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> documents in the initial ranking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since each query yields a ranking, and the dataset consists of multiple queries, the average NDCG and NDCG@k with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math> over queries will be computed as final evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Results and Discussion</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each query, all the passages in relation with it have been ranked, and the NDCG, NDCG@5 and NDCG@10 has been computed for the rank yielded by each model. Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ LLM-RJS ‣ 4 Experiment 1 ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the results of Experiment 1 as a violin plot with NDCG, NDCG@5 and NDCG@10 of all models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The NERS and LLM-RJS models performed on par with previous models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hofstätter et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xing et al., 2023</a></cite>. These previous models are purposely trained dense retrievers, unlike the models analyzed by this paper, which are zero-shot. Note that the zero-shot models seem to outperform trained retrievers. The presented NDCG@10 of these previous models was reported in the respective papers and not implemented here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For this experiment, only Llama3.1:8b, Llama3.2:1b and Sonnet 4.5 had any failures, out of which, Llama3.2:1bs and Sonnet 4.5 have been excluded for having failed more than 2/3 of all data points (lack of instruction compliance, see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompt ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix D Examples of LLM-RJS failure ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> provides an example of failure for each model.). The models failed to only output one integer between 0 and 3. Expectedly, one of the excluded models is Llama3.2:1b, the smallest model in the selection, with 8312/9260 failed responses. Surprisingly, the other one was Sonnet 4.5, the state-of-the-art (at the time of writing) of Anthropic, which failed to only give relevance scores for 7473/9260 data points. Llama3.1:8b only failed 84/9260 data points.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.08077v1/reasoning_experiment_overview.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/133;" width="476" height="133"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Overview of Experiment 2. The LLM estimates relevance of (query, document) pairs in "high reasoning" and "low reasoning" conditions. The mean absolute error between LLM and human assessment in the two conditions is used as evaluation metric. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, NERS are still most proficient for ranking documents, and perform the task better for only a fraction of the cost, and a fraction of the time. The price per 1 million tokens strongly favors NERS, with Gemini Embeddings costing 0.15$ per 1 million tokens. On the other side, GPT-5.1 costs 1.25$ per 1 million input tokens and 10$ per 1 million output tokens. Albeit financially undesirable, LLMs do not leave much on the table, when it comes to ranking (query, document) pairs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The closed-source models consistently outperform the open models. The bottom line is, if one is looking for the best option, Gemini Embeddings is the winning model. If, aside performance, running the retrieval system locally is also a consideration, nomic-embed-text:v1.5 is a viable option.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
