<td id="S5.T3.2.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.14</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.10.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.10.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">exons definition biology</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.10.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T3.2.10.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">A portion of DNA that codes for a section of the mature messenger RNA obtained from that DNA […]</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">0</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">3</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">2</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.10.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">0.55</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.11.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.11.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T3.2.11.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">how long is life cycle of flea</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.11.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T3.2.11.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">[…] <span id="S5.T3.2.11.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adults live about 7 to 10 days and deposit eggs on the moist soil surface or in soil cracks</span> […]</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">0</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">3</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.11.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">2</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.11.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.11.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.38</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.12" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.12.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.12.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">is cdg airport in main paris</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.12.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T3.2.12.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">It is located near Roissy, 25 km to the north-east of Paris. The construction of this airport lasted about 10 years […]</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">0</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.12.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">3</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.12.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">2</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.12.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;">0.47</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>First 10 query-IDs where GPT5.1 gave rating 3 while the humans gave 0. The annotations suffer from short-sightedness, while GPT-5.1 with reasoning correctly identifies relevance. Of the 94 such cases only 5 were genuine false positives (shown in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Genuine False Positives in GPT 5.1 with reasoning ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). The NERS similarity averages 0.44 for these 94 cases, while it is 0.50 for true positives (higher is more similar). <span id="S5.T3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> highlights key examples of the short-sightedness limitation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Chain of Thought (CoT) reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a></cite> has been shown to yield substantial improvement in LLM capabilities on a variety of tasks. It works by letting the LLM generate a longer series of tokens as “reasoning” before giving the final answer. This strategy has been shown to lead to better results in image Information Retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and is therefore also promising for having LLM-RJS outperform NERS models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We prompt GPT-5.1 to estimate the relevance of (document, query) pairs with the prompt described in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompt ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. To compare the reasoning against the non-reasoning conditions we change the ‘‘reasoning effort’’<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/reasoning#get-started-with-reasoning</span></span></span>, which controls the amount of reasoning tokens before the output. A visual overview of the experiment is shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiment 1 ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The same experiment was also performed with gpt-oss:20b, a smaller open-source model, with similar results reported in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Reasoning experiment with GPT-OSS ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since the LLM-RJS can directly estimate prompt relevance under the same definitions as the human annotations, the relevance estimates are compared directly, instead of by the ranking. Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5 Experiment 2 ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the confusion matrices of the non-reasoning and reasoning LLM-RJS relevance rating compared to human relevance ratings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We observe reasonable agreement between the LLM-RJS systems and the human annotations, but find that in many cases the LLM-RJS assigns higher relevance ratings than the humans annotations. This is particularly visible in the large number of cases where the human rating has relevance 0 (irrelevant), while the LLM-RJS has relevance 1 (related). This difference increases for the reasoning model, which identifies yet more <span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">(document, query)</span> pairs as related. These higher ratings are also reflected in the average relevance ratings, where the average human rating is 0.79, LLM-RJS without reasoning is 1.03, and with reasoning is 1.23.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.08077v1/cm_no_reasoning_gpt5.1.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:163/165;" width="163" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F5.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.08077v1/cm_reasoning_gpt5.1.png" id="S5.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/165;" width="143" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Differences between human and LLM relevance ratings with and without reasoning for GPT 5.1. The LLMs overall are more likely to see relevance in documents, especially with reasoning.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Particularly interesting is that the reasoning configuration, which would be expected to perform better, has double the incidences where human ratings identify a pair as irrelevant (0), while the LLM-RJS identifies the pair as perfectly relevant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Experiment 2 ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows examples from these cases. Predominantly, the passages are actually highly relevant, but might not be immediately obvious. The “difference between a mcdouble and a double cheeseburger” is indeed one slice of cheese instead of two. This may not be obvious to human annotators or short-sighted NERS models that evaluate similarity, but in these cases the reasoning LLM-RJS outperforms the benchmark.
Overall the results from Experiment 2 show that the addition of reasoning allows the model to identify relevance that is not obvious, beyond the human annotations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We proposed that the short-sightedness problem of NERS may be resolved with relevance reasoning LLM-RJS. Similiarity is not always sufficient for relevance. In Experiment 1 we evaluated whether these LLM-RJS outperform NERS on a ranking task, and do not find improvement. In Experiment 2 we added reasoning, and directly compared the LLM-RJS relevance rankings to the human rankings. This showed the the LLM-RJS was able to identify more documents as relevant than the human annotations. This suggests that the human annotations suffer from similar short-sightedness as the NERS systems, explaining why the LLM-RJS do not reach a noticeable improvement compared to NERS when evaluated on NDCG with short-sighted human annotations as ground truth.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The primary limitation to LLM-RJS systems is the added cost compared to embedding models. The price-per-token of top performing LLMs is roughly 10x that of equivalent embedding models. Additionally, for NERS systems implemented at scale, each document only needs to be embedded once even for multiple queries, and all document embeddings can be stored in a vector database for efficient retrieval. The LLM-RJS system demonstrated here requires LLM inference for each (query, document) pair and therefore does not scale well. A solution may be found in CoT re-ranking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, where an embedding model is used to make a first selection of documents that are similar, and then the reasoning-LLM selects the relevant documents from the subset of similar documents. However, this only addresses the problem where similar documents are not relevant, but would still fail in cases as the example in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where the document is relevant but not similar.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we should consider that the short-sightedness in human annotations is not universal for all datasets. We have shown cases of short-sightedness in this dataset, but through expert annotators, instructions that support further research, and more time for annotations it is possible to construct relevance annotations that do not suffer from short-sightedness. Since LLM-RJS models do not need large training datasets, efforts can be focused on only creating test sets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Future work</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The presented results suggest, but do not definitively show that LLM-RJS models can outperform human annotations. To establish the performance difference between human relevance and LLM-RJS relevance re-annotation may be done where trained annotators compare existing labels and LLM-RJS relevance judgements and establish which is correct. This can be supported by additionally providing the reasoning chain from the LLM-RJS, to provide argumentation for the judgment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We have established in this instance of an Information Retrieval a mismatch between similarity and relevance, and show this as a nearsightedness-bottleneck for embeddings. However, for a variety of different Information Retrieval tasks, a variety of retrieval requirements exist beyond ‘relevance’. In tasks further conditions apply such as preferring high-quality documents, while in other tasks specifically similarity might be preferred over relevance (e.g. for detecting copyright infringement). With advanced Information Retrieval capabilities of LLM-RJS models, soft requirements can be considered, and there are more possibilities to deviate from ‘similarity’ or requirements that can be quantified. Evaluating LLM-RJS against human annotations still appears to be the standard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arabzadeh and Clarke (2025)</a></cite>, and efforts are needed to re-specify what the (sub)tasks of Information Retrieval problems are, and how LLM-RJS models can be evaluated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, we found that the short-sightedness motivation presented in the illustrative Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is a valid limitation of NERS models that can be addressed by LLM-RJS. However, as shown with the examples of short-sighted annotations in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Experiment 2 ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, human annotations suffer from the same short-sightedness limitation, they present as false-positives for a confusion matrix in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5 Experiment 2 ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Therefore, under a standard annotated benchmark evaluation as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ LLM-RJS ‣ 4 Experiment 1 ‣ Why Large Language Models can Secretly Outperform Embedding Similarity in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, they do not demonstrably outperform NERS, while they still could be giving better relevance judgments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Declaration on Generative AI:</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For this paper, OpenAi’s GPT-5, GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2 models were used for minor reformulations and spelling checking, and LaTeX formatting.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
